our next speaker is michael kim uh  michael just completed his phd in the  stanford theory group  and he's not currently a uc berkeley  miller post doctor fellow  michael great um wait for the slide  great so we can go to the next slide um  thanks so these days predictive  algorithms are everywhere we look  they drive our cars and our economies  they're making medical predictions  and even sometimes telling us who we  should get married to  um and really algorithms are  increasingly used to make consequential  decisions about people and are having a  profound  impact on our daily lives and  while such predictive algorithms have  the potential to yield great benefits to  society  unfortunately for every amazing  algorithmic achievement you hear about  you also see like on the next slide  headlines  highlighting the bias and unfair  discrimination that can be baked into  these predictive algorithms  on the next side we discuss a specific  issue that came into focus recently in  the california elections  where prop 25 gave voters a choice to  eliminate  cash bail and replace it with a certain  risk assessment algorithm  now cash bail has long been understood  to discriminate against people on the  basis  of race and socioeconomics but critics  of the proposition  like the aclu of socal worried that the  risk assessment algorithm would exhibit  exactly the same issues of bias and  unfair discrimination  as cash bail itself and so it emits  lots of concern about bias and  prediction systems  it's actually quite easy to identify  examples where  the algorithms we're using are behaving  in a manner that somehow we feel is  unfair  but devising an effective strategy for  mitigating this algorithmic unfairness  is a bit more challenging  and that's where my research begins  we'll talk about it on the next slide  so rather than addressing each example  of bias as we see it on the fly  my research tries to take a more  foundational approach identifying  general patterns of unfair  algorithmic behavior and then  translating these patterns into formal  notions of fairness  in the language of theoretical computer  science and really a large focus of the  research  is on making sure that we have the right  definitions where we need to balance the  desire for general purpose solutions  as well as an awareness for the context  in which the notions will be applied  and ideally developing these definitions  will really help us to understand  what we can and cannot expect from  machine learned predictions  in a given context the eventual goal of  the research program is to develop  efficient machine learning that comes  with a provable guarantee of fairness  properties  much in the same way that modern  cryptography provides us in our browser  with a provable certificate  of security in our online transactions  on the next slide i want to give you a  flavor of the type of definitions that  we work with  so the main notion that we've been  studying for the past few years is  called multi-calibration  multi-calibration  says that predictions should appear  calibrated  not just overall but on every  sub-population that can be identified  within some computationally bounded  class of sub-populations  we show that the guarantees of  multi-calibration are significantly  stronger  than the corresponding guarantees of  group fairness  and we give algorithms that reduce the  problem of learning a multi-calibrated  predictor  to standard machine learning tasks  showing its feasibility  so finally on the last slide i want to  conclude  with a preview of some hot off the press  work where we start with a really simple  idea  and the idea is that perhaps fair  predictions  should somehow be indistinguishable from  a set of ideal predictions  now you have to be careful about exactly  how you make this intuition rigorous  but it leads us to a certain notion that  we called outcome to  indistinguishability  the framework outcome in  distinguishability for reasoning about  predictions  turns out to be very general even more  general than multi-calibration  and sets up a number of interesting  questions about learning and prediction  that we're excited to dig into further  thanks a lot
 

